WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.852
Oh great. Another whiteboard app.

2
00:00:03.452 --> 00:00:05.351
Yeah. But this one is different.

3
00:00:08.451 --> 00:00:11.755
Start with an empty board, and tell it what you want, in one line.

4
00:00:15.255 --> 00:00:16.421
That's all it needs.

5
00:00:18.621 --> 00:00:23.129
Every box, every arrow, laid out for you. And nothing lands until you place it.

6
00:00:23.929 --> 00:00:25.952
Okay. That's actually neat.

7
00:00:26.652 --> 00:00:29.170
Every diagram has two things you can change.

8
00:00:29.570 --> 00:00:35.269
Its structure: how the parts are arranged. In tiers, around a hub, or as a pipeline.

9
00:00:36.869 --> 00:00:42.675
And its look: how it's drawn. Watercolour, blueprint, ink, and forty more.

10
00:00:44.275 --> 00:00:47.509
Change either one, and the same diagram redraws itself.

11
00:00:48.709 --> 00:00:50.320
And when the design changes?

12
00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:56.187
Just say so. Ask for a CDN in front of the gateway, and it grows the diagram you already have.

13
00:00:58.587 --> 00:01:03.194
Changes to your work arrive as a list. Tick what you want, hold to compare, then apply.

14
00:01:05.794 --> 00:01:06.914
I've got the gateway.

15
00:01:07.064 --> 00:01:08.664
I'll take the database.

16
00:01:09.014 --> 00:01:13.003
Your whole team works on the same board, live. Even inside the same note.

17
00:01:15.403 --> 00:01:18.249
Built something worth keeping? Save it as a piece.

18
00:01:19.049 --> 00:01:22.832
Drop it into any board, share it with your team, or publish it for everyone.

19
00:01:26.032 --> 00:01:28.070
And the board remembers every change.

20
00:01:29.470 --> 00:01:30.366
Rewind it.

21
00:01:34.566 --> 00:01:37.774
Branch it, to try an idea without touching the original.

22
00:01:42.374 --> 00:01:44.458
And merge it back, once it works.

23
00:01:50.058 --> 00:01:51.890
So it's Git, for whiteboards.

24
00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:52.995
Exactly.

25
00:01:55.195 --> 00:01:56.646
Wait. What did I miss?

26
00:01:56.996 --> 00:02:00.837
Ask it to catch you up, and it tells you what changed while you were away.

27
00:02:03.837 --> 00:02:09.122
Point it at a GitHub repository, and it draws the frontend, the backend and the database for you.

28
00:02:11.122 --> 00:02:18.204
Even your AI agents can join. Claude, Cursor or VS Code can read the board, and draw on it, right beside you.

29
00:02:20.604 --> 00:02:26.379
Fifty thousand elements on one board, and each frame still draws in three and a half milliseconds.

30
00:02:27.579 --> 00:02:30.154
Export it, embed it, or share a link.

31
00:02:35.354 --> 00:02:36.897
Alright. I'm sold.

32
00:02:37.497 --> 00:02:40.331
Mosaic. Say it, and it's on the board.
